PCON(1)                        ZOAU Command Syntax                       PCON(1)

NAME

	pcon - Print the contents of the syslog in a given timeframe.

SYNOPSIS

	pcon [-hjJv] [-S <SYSTEM NAME>* | -A] <TIME FRAME>

		Where <TIME FRAME> is one of:
			[-adlmrwy*]			 Short options for relative timeframes
			-t <MINUTES>		 Other specific relative timeframe
			-s <yyddd hh:mm:ss>	 Specific system time

	-A, --allsys
		Print syslog records from all systems.

	-a, -*, --all
		Print the entire log.

	-d, --day
		Print the previous 24 hours of the log.

	--debug
		Print debug information.

	-h, --help
		Print syntax help.

	-j, --json
		Print output in JSON format with this structure:
		{
			"data": {
				"SYSTEM_A": {
					"content": "...",
					"content_length": 0
				},
				"SYSTEM_B": {...},
			},
			"program": "pcon",
			"options": "-j ",
			"rc": "0"
		}

		If the log is empty for the time frame requested, the value for
		"content" is a single space " " and "content_length" is 0.

	-J, --prettyjson
		Print JSON output in a readable format.
		This option forces -j if it was not set.

	-l, --hour
		Print the previous 60 minutes of the log.

	-m, --month
		Print the previous 30 days of the log.

	-r, --recent
		Print the previous 10 minutes of the log.

	-s, --since
		Print the log from a given Julian date (yyddd) and
		time (hh:mm:ss). If the seconds are omitted, 00 is the default.

	-S, --system
		Print syslog from the specified system name.
		A comma-separated list of system names can be passed to print records
		from multiple systems, or '*' for all systems in a sysplex.

	-t, --timediff
		Print the log from a specified number of minutes ago.
		For example, to print the log from 45 minutes ago up to the present,
		enter 'pcon -t 45'. To print from 5.5 hours ago to the present, enter
		'pcon -t 330'.

	-v, --verbose
		Print verbose information.

	-w, --week
		Print the previous 7 days of the log.

	-y, --year
		Print the previous 365 days of the log.

DESCRIPTION

	pcon prints the syslog. The prior 10 minutes of the log are printed by
	default. The following options can be specified:

	* A particular date and time.

	* A relative duration.

	* The entire log.

	To specify a particular date, you must use the Julian date format, which is the
	day-of-year number. For example, to specify a date of February 15, use
	day-of-year number "046".

	The output format is described in the z/OS documentation for
	"HCL Programming Interface Information"
	(https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/zos/latest?topic=iar-hcl-information)
	and "SYSLOG records"
	(https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/zos/latest?topic=automation-syslog-records).

NOTES

	pcon is incompatible with OPERLOG. For pcon to display console messages, set
	the z/OS console hardcopy medium to SYSLOG.

	To determine the hardcopy medium on a system, issue the following z/OS
	console command:

		DISPLAY CONSOLES,HARDCOPY

	If the response message CNZ4100I contains "LOG=(OPERLOG)", pcon does
	not function correctly on this system. If the response is "LOG=(SYSLOG)",
	"LOG=(SYSLOG,OPERLOG)", or similar, pcon displays the syslog output.

	For more information, see "z/OS MVS System Messages, Vol 4 (CBD-DMO)"
	(https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/zos/latest?topic=messages-cnz4100i)
	and "z/OS MVS System Commands"
	(https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/zos/latest?topic=command-displaying-console-status-information-consoles)

	All relevant records from each syslog are printed at a time, so chronology
	between messages from different systems is lost. The order of the system
	names in the -S option is not maintained in the output.
	The order corresponds to the ID of each SYSLOG job in ascending order.

EXAMPLES

	Print the entire log:

		pcon -a

	Print the last hour of the log:

		pcon -l

	Print the last two minutes of the log. This can be useful for
	systems with large amounts of log activity.

		pcon -t 2

	Print the log from 22:17:33 on 03/23/21. This is the 82nd day
	of 2021:

		pcon -s 21082 22:17:33

EXIT VALUES

	0
		pcon completed successfully.

	2
		Syntax error. Correct the pcon syntax and resubmit the command.

	4
		An invalid argument was specified. Examine the error message,
		correct the argument, and resubmit the command.

	8
		An error occurred when the log information was retrieved.
		Examine the error message for details.
